-- title:
--   Ideals saturated with respect to t are principal
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring that is Noetherian and local, let $S$ be a commutative ring that is a domain and a discrete valuation ring, and let $f \colon R \to S$ be a ring homomorphism. Assume $f$ is surjective, and let $t \in R$ be an element with $\ker f = (t)$, the ideal generated by $t$. Let $P$ be an ideal of $R$ which is saturated with respect to $t$, in the sense that for every $r \in R$ with $tr \in P$ one has $r \in P$ (equivalently $(P : t) = P$). The conclusion is that $P$ is principal as an $R$-submodule of $R$, i.e. there exists $a \in R$ with $P = (a)$. Note that $a$ is not named in the conclusion, which asserts only the existence of a single generator; no hypothesis of regularity, of factoriality, or on the dimension of $R$ is imposed, and $P$ is not assumed prime.
--
--   This is the local algebra input used to produce principal generators of ideals on a curve from information at a single closed point: it says that a $t$-saturated ideal in a Noetherian local ring presented as a discrete valuation ring modulo a principal ideal is principal. It is applied in the project to pass from an ideal of a local ring of a two-chart model to a principal ideal with a generator that is a non-zero-divisor, via [`Ideal.exists_eq_span_singleton_and_mem_nonZeroDivisors_of_forall_mul_mem_of_surjective_of_isDiscreteValuationRing`](thm.html#Ideal.exists_eq_span_singleton_and_mem_nonZeroDivisors_of_forall_mul_mem_of_surjective_of_isDiscreteValuationRing) and its use for the modular curve $X_1$.
